## 1. Absorbing-target synchronization is exactly a PFA value problem

Let \(\Sigma=\{P_a:a\in A\}\) be rational row-stochastic matrices on a finite state set \(Q\).

Fix:

- an initial distribution \(p\);
- a distinguished target state \(z\);
- assume \(z\) is absorbing for every generator:
  \[
  e_zP_a=e_z.
  \]

Define

\[
v_z(\Sigma,p)
=
\inf_{w\in A^\ast}
TV(pP_w,e_z).
\]

Because the second distribution remains the point mass \(e_z\),

\[
TV(r,e_z)=1-r(z)
\]

for every probability distribution \(r\).

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
v_z(\Sigma,p)
=
1-
\sup_{w\in A^\ast}(pP_w)(z).
}
\]

Thus an absorbing-target synchronization problem is literally the value problem of a probabilistic automaton with initial distribution \(p\) and accepting set \(\{z\}\).

Consequences:

\[
\boxed{
v_z=0
\iff
\operatorname{val}(\mathcal A)=1.
}
\]

More generally, for every \(\varepsilon\in(0,1)\),

\[
\boxed{
\exists w:\ TV(pP_w,e_z)<\varepsilon
\iff
\exists w:\ (pP_w)(z)>1-\varepsilon.
}
\]

So PFA value/cutpoint theory is not merely analogous to one synchronization problem; it is exactly the same problem under this absorbing-target contract.

