## 2. Leaktightness gives a decidable synchronization island

Fijalkow–Gimbert–Kelmendi–Oualhadj prove that the value-1 problem is decidable, in fact PSPACE-complete over the full class, when the probabilistic automaton is leaktight.

Therefore:

### Theorem CW-002G.1 — Leaktight absorbing-target synchronization

If the absorbing-target PFA \((Q,A,P,p,\{z\})\) is leaktight, then deciding

\[
\boxed{
\inf_wTV(pP_w,e_z)=0
}
\]

is decidable in PSPACE.

The Markov monoid supplies a finite asymptotic certificate.

No claim is made here that the absorbing-singleton-target restriction itself is PSPACE-hard.

---

## 3. Value-1 witnesses become synchronization witnesses

A limit-word \(u:Q^2\to\{0,1\}\) is a value-1 witness when every asymptotically surviving state reachable from the initial state is accepting.

For the singleton accepting set \(\{z\}\), this reduces to

\[
\boxed{
u(p,t)=1
\Longrightarrow
t=z
}
\]

in the obvious initial-state version.

Thus, in absorbing-target language, a Markov-monoid witness is an asymptotic support certificate saying:

> every state carrying nonvanishing mass after the witnessing word sequence has collapsed into the target.

For leaktight automata, completeness of the Markov monoid makes this certificate necessary as well as sufficient.

---

## 4. What a leak really is: support Booleanization fails to commute with a limit

The extended Markov monoid tracks two Boolean relations:

- \(u(s,t)=1\): transition probability has a strictly positive limit;
- \(u^+(s,t)=1\): transition probability remains positive for every finite member of the realizing sequence.

For a convergent sequence of stochastic products \(P_n\to P\), these are the two operations

\[
u
=
\mathbf 1_{\{P>0\}},
\]

and schematically

\[
u^+
=
\mathbf 1_{\{P_n>0\text{ for all }n\}}.
\]

Always,

\[
u\le u^+,
\]

but equality can fail because

\[
x_n>0\ \forall n,
\qquad
x_n\to0.
\]

A leak witness is exactly a recurrent edge for which

\[
\boxed{
u(r,q)=0,
\qquad
u^+(r,q)=1.
}
\]

In words:

> the Boolean support says the edge is present at every finite stage, while the actual asymptotic stochastic weight says the edge disappears.

Fijalkow et al.'s algebraic characterization is:

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal A\text{ is leaktight}
\iff
\text{its extended Markov monoid contains no leak witness}.
}
\]

Hence leaktightness can be read as exclusion of a particular **recurrent support/limit noncommutation defect**.

This is an exact restatement of the classical definition, but it explains the support-versus-weight phase transition found in CW-002E.

