## 5. Stabilization \(u^\#\) is an asymptotic-support regularizer

For an idempotent Boolean limit-word \(u\), the Markov monoid iteration is

\[
\boxed{
u^\#(s,t)=1
\iff
u(s,t)=1
\text{ and }t\text{ is }u\text{-recurrent}.
}
\]

So \(u^\#\) deletes edges that lead only to transient states.

This is precisely the Boolean support expected after iterating the stochastic behavior for increasingly long times.

Thus the \(\#\)-operation can be interpreted as a finite algebraic regularization of naïve support:

\[
\text{finite support}
\longrightarrow
\text{asymptotically recurrent support}.
\]

The extended monoid then keeps \(u^+\) as a memory of finite positive edges so that disappearing "leak" edges are not silently forgotten.

