## 6. Why ordinary Boolean support is insufficient

CW-002E found:

- global all-row synchronization to zero can be decided from finite Boolean support products;
- designated-pair synchronization can be undecidable.

Leaktight theory explains the mechanism.

Boolean multiplication remembers only:

\[
P_w(i,j)>0?
\]

It cannot distinguish

\[
10^{-3},10^{-100},10^{-10^{10}}
\]

from one another.

Value-1 behavior depends precisely on probabilities that may remain positive at every finite stage yet vanish asymptotically.

Leaks formalize this pathology.

Thus the support abstraction fails not because it is wrong at finite time, but because

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{supp}(\lim P_n)
\ne
\lim \operatorname{supp}(P_n)
}
\]

in the relevant asymptotic sense.

Leaktightness is a structural regime where the stabilization-monoid correction is complete enough to recover the value-1 answer.

---

## 7. Connection to profinite completion

Fijalkow's prostochastic framework later recasts value-1 as an emptiness problem in a profinite/topological completion of the free stochastic word monoid.

This suggests three distinct synchronization completions:

1. Boolean support semigroup — exact finite reachability, too coarse for vanishing weights;
2. Markov/stabilization monoid — finite asymptotic abstraction, complete on leaktight systems;
3. prostochastic/profinite completion — topological limit object for arbitrary word sequences.

This mirrors the broader CWR principle:

\[
\boxed{
\text{when finite objects fail to contain limits, enlarge the semantic category before declaring the problem nonexistent}.
}
\]

---

# Verdict

Absorbing-target stochastic synchronization is exactly a probabilistic-automaton value problem.

Leaktightness is therefore not merely transferable to synchronization; it already is an intrinsic decidability condition for that synchronization contract.

Its conceptual content can be summarized as:

\[
\boxed{
\text{exclude recurrent positive edges that survive every finite support but vanish in the limit}.
}
\]

The next question is to generalize this "support-limit defect" criterion from a single absorbing target to arbitrary designated-pair coalescence.
